[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  tuesday august 16th i'm zoe thomas for  the wall street journal  ever since apple rolled out its ios 14.5  last year making it easier for users to  restrict what data apps could collect  facebook has had a problem  the company and rivals like twitter and  snapchat have seen their ad revenue drop  that's because their ability to target  advertising to users has been hampered  by apple's decision to let users opt out  of having their online activity tracked  facebook and apple have been at odds  ever since  but the wall street journal has  exclusively learned that apple and  facebook back before it changed its name  to meta were once engaged in secret  conversations about a potential  partnership including revenue sharing  arrangements joining us to discuss what  the companies talked about and why it  didn't work out is our reporter salvador  rodriguez hi sal thanks for being here  yeah thank you so much for having me  so let's start with your reporting found  that this feud may be between apple and  facebook might not have come about what  kinds of things were the two companies  discussing before apple put in its  privacy policies with its ios 14.5 yeah  what we found in our reporting is that  before apple introduced ios 14 and and  really revolutionized the digital ad  industry  before all that came to be apple and  facebook had discussions over several  years about a number of different ways  that the two companies could work  together that would actually  give some of facebook's revenue over to  apple by way of the standard 30 cut that  apple takes from its app developers  that's according to people familiar with  the discussions  okay so what were the ways that apple  was trying to get at some of that  revenue yeah so basically this was all  happening during a time when apple was  really trying to ramp up its services  business and one way to do that was by  getting folks to add more in-app  purchases to their apps or  create features or business models that  included subscription revenue so we  understand that some of the suggestions  that were made were gifting for creators  but more notably  there was a talk of creating a  subscription-based ad-free facebook  the other thing that was also haggled  over between the two companies was over  a very specific facebook product called  boosted posts  there was some on the apple side who  argued that this product was not an  advertisement but rather an in-app  purchase  and if apple had ultimately decided that  it was an in-app purchase that would  have entitled the iphone maker to 30  of the revenue generated by facebook on  that particular product  what was facebook's reaction to these  proposals for example to having you know  boosting be considered an in-app  purchase or even to starting a  subscription service for facebook  from my reporting it's my understanding  that the  subscription-based facebook was  something that was  thought of but never really seriously  considered it just goes completely  against what facebook has always argued  it goes completely against their  advertising business when it comes to  boosted posts they were very steadfast  that that was an advertising product and  apple does not take advertising revenue  from the facebook side they argued that  even though this is something that you  can purchase through your smartphone a  lot of times these are small businesses  that are purchasing ads in this manner  it might be a food truck owner  who isn't really running their business  out of a laptop but still needs to  advertise and doing it through their  smartphone just makes the most sense for  that particular advertiser  they also argue that just like with  other ads in their system when you place  this ad you have to determine how many  people you would like to see this ad for  how long and it even goes into the  bidding system so it's competing against  other ads so they argued and held firm  that boosted posts are advertisements  they are not in-app purchases okay so  facebook didn't bite on those offers but  has apple had these kinds of  conversations maybe these proposals to  other companies apart from facebook well  that's the big question that's certainly  um what a lot of people are wondering  about  we do know that apple has a deal in  place with google that makes google the  default search engine  for the safari browser on  the iphone  and it's something where google pays  apple billions of dollars annually to  hold that ground  and after our story went out we heard  from at least one developer  who said that they found themselves in a  similar situation as facebook  with a boosted post feature of their own  and in that particular case their app  was rejected until they implemented that  feature as an in-app purchase and gave  apple the 30 percent cut  one of the companies said in response to  your reporting what have you heard from  apple and from facebook facebook didn't  contest our findings especially in  regards to their relationship with apple  and apple meanwhile what they had to say  is that they have these type of  conversations with  all sorts of developers all the time  no matter how big or small developers  might be  they usually go in and meet with  developers and they tend to make  suggestions for different business  models that they see working throughout  their app store ecosystem so what they  argued is that this is not out of the  ordinary and this was certainly not a  quid pro quo  you know this position on on ads on kind  of privacy versus personalization it  really has created this uh disparity  between how different big tech companies  sort of see the digital world where is  it leaving the relationship between  facebook and apple now  it really redefines the relationship  between the two because as these privacy  changes were coming out there was a very  sort of moral argument that was  happening with apple saying that you  know user privacy that's what matters  most that's what we're all about that's  what we're trying to protect  with these changes and facebook arguing  that we think personalized ads are what  consumers want and we think that these  type of ads help small businesses that's  how it was framed in the time  immediately after the change and in the  time right before it however now knowing  that these two companies considered  working together they certainly talked  about it there were back and forth it  really just makes you wonder  what would the digital ad industry look  like today  if perhaps they had come to some sort of  agreement that did work for  both all right that was our reporter  salvador rodriguez thanks so much for  joining us sal thank you as always  and that's it for today's tech news  briefing if you want more tech stories  check out our website wsj.com  and if you like our show please rate and  review it you can do that wherever you  get your podcasts  i'm zoe thomas for the wall street  journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  you

